(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-bronchodilators-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Asthma & the Bronchodilators Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug group is used to reverse acute bronchospasm in asthma?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Mast cell stabilizers","Leukotriene modifiers","Short-acting beta-2 agonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchospasm is prevented by avoiding triggers and reversed with short-acting beta-2 agonists, which work rapidly and last 4 to 6 hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Beta-2 agonists relax airway smooth muscle by increasing which intracellular messenger?","choices":["Cyclic guanosine monophosphate","Cyclic adenosine monophosphate","Intracellular calcium","Acetylcholine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonists increase cyclic adenosine monophosphate, which inhibits myosin light chain kinase and relaxes airway smooth muscle. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Besides short-acting beta-2 agonists and systemic corticosteroids, which drug group is a quick-relief asthma medication?","choices":["Leukotriene modifiers","Mast cell stabilizers","Anticholinergics","Methylxanthines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The quick-relief medications are short-acting beta-2 agonists, anticholinergics and systemic corticosteroids. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"When allergen binds immunoglobulin E on a mast cell, which mediator is released immediately from the granules?","choices":["Histamine","Leukotrienes","Interleukins","Prostaglandins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Granule content release is immediate: histamine, proteases, heparin and tumor necrosis factor. Lipid mediators follow over minutes and cytokines over hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"At toxic levels, which central nervous system effect of theophylline is the danger?","choices":["Drowsiness","Emergence phenomenon","Convulsions","Peripheral neuritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline is a central nervous system stimulant, and at high levels it causes convulsions; as a cardiac stimulant it also causes arrhythmias. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Besides its limited use in asthma, theophylline is used in which condition?","choices":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Acute bronchitis","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline (available only as a generic) has limited use and is used in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Besides tremor and tachycardia, which metabolic adverse effect is listed for beta-2 agonists?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Hyperglycemia","Hyperuricemia","Hyperthermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonist adverse effects are tachycardia, hypotension, headache, dizziness, insomnia, tremors and hyperglycemia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which drug group reduces the level of bronchial responsiveness in asthma over the long term?","choices":["Short-acting beta-2 agonists","Anticholinergics","Histamine H1 blockers","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial responsiveness is related to airway inflammation and is reduced long term with anti-inflammatory agents such as inhaled corticosteroids. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Why are theophylline plasma levels monitored?","choices":["It induces liver enzymes","It has a narrow therapeutic index","It causes adrenal suppression","Its effect takes 8 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index, so plasma levels are monitored; rising levels bring nausea, vomiting and cardiac effects, then seizures and arrhythmias. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which change involving urine is listed for theophylline?","choices":["Urinary retention","Orange discoloration","A diuretic effect","Less uric acid excretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline has diuretic effects, alongside gastric acid stimulation, smooth muscle relaxation and central nervous system and cardiac stimulation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"In airway smooth muscle, the cyclic adenosine monophosphate produced after beta-2 receptor stimulation inhibits which enzyme?","choices":["Phosphodiesterase","Myosin light chain kinase","Myosin light chain phosphatase","5-lipoxygenase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The beta-2 receptor acts through the stimulatory G protein to make cyclic adenosine monophosphate, which inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so the myosin light chain is not phosphorylated and the muscle relaxes. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How do ipratropium and tiotropium cause bronchodilation?","choices":["They stimulate beta-2 receptors","They block muscarinic receptors","They inhibit phosphodiesterase","They stabilize mast cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticholinergics block acetylcholine's effect on muscarinic receptors, which produces bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A patient develops flushing, fever, dry mouth and a fast heart rate after taking an anticholinergic. Which other finding belongs to the same toxicity pattern?","choices":["Diarrhea","Excess salivation","Bradycardia","Urinary retention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticholinergic toxicity also affects the bowel and bladder, giving constipation and urinary retention, alongside flushing, fever, dry mouth and tachycardia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Ipratropium (Atrovent) belongs to which drug class?","choices":["Anticholinergics","Beta-2 agonists","Methylxanthines","Mast cell stabilizers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ipratropium is the short-acting anticholinergic: it blocks acetylcholine's effect on muscarinic receptors, causing bronchodilation, and lasts up to 8 hours. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which effect on the stomach is listed among theophylline's adverse effects?","choices":["Blockade of gastric acid","Constipation","Oral thrush","Stimulation of gastric acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline stimulates gastric acid, and nausea and heartburn are on the methylxanthine adverse effect list. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which pair of adverse effects is listed for beta-2 agonists as a class?","choices":["Thrush and growth suppression","Dry mouth and urinary retention","Increased heart rate and tremor","Convulsions and arrhythmias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-2 agonists increase cyclic adenosine monophosphate and relax airway smooth muscle, but they cause increased heart rate and tremor. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Besides the direct effect of released mediators, stimulation of which airway receptors initiates reflex bronchoconstriction in asthma?","choices":["Afferent vagal receptors","Beta-2 adrenergic receptors","Adenosine A2B receptors","Mast cell immunoglobulin E receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Released mediators also stimulate afferent vagal receptors in the airways to initiate reflex bronchoconstriction, and tachykinins such as substance P directly contract smooth muscle. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 63"}]};
